1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,964
Heat dissipation is thermal energy spreading from a hotter region to a cooler one.

2
00:00:04,964 --> 00:00:08,851
A freshly baked pie cools as its heat flows into the cooler air.

3
00:00:08,851 --> 00:00:11,961
An ice pack on an injury absorbs heat from the skin.

4
00:00:13,020 --> 00:00:19,599
An open system exchanges both energy and matter with its surroundings; a closed system exchanges only energy.

5
00:00:19,599 --> 00:00:25,340
Insulation, as in a vacuum flask, slows the flow of heat, keeping drinks hot or cold for longer.

6
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:28,134
Nature uses dissipation too.

7
00:00:28,134 --> 00:00:33,456
An African elephant's huge, thin ears give a large surface area, so blood cools quickly.

8
00:00:33,456 --> 00:00:38,061
And phones and laptops are designed to dissipate heat so they don't overheat.

9
00:00:39,121 --> 00:00:43,786
A cup of freshly made tea is left on a table in a room at 22 degrees Celsius.

10
00:00:43,786 --> 00:00:46,537
Its temperature is measured every few minutes.

11
00:00:47,596 --> 00:00:54,055
In the first five minutes the tea cools by 16 degrees. Between 30 and 40 minutes it cools by only 5 degrees.

12
00:00:55,115 --> 00:00:59,241
The curve gets less steep as the tea gets closer to room temperature.

13
00:01:00,301 --> 00:01:04,427
Pause and decide: why does the tea cool more slowly as time goes on,

14
00:01:04,427 --> 00:01:06,999
and what temperature will it finally reach?
